WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.700
How do you map every neuron in a piece of brain?

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.300
In the inner retina, amacrine cells tangle with ganglion cells, the retina's output

3
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:15.320
neurons. A single slice shows only flat patches where branches cross the cut.

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.980
To trace who meets whom, you must reconstruct the whole volume.

5
00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:28.073
Serial block-face electron microscopy builds that volume step by step.

6
00:00:29.253 --> 00:00:31.213
Start with a small block of mouse retina.

7
00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:36.713
The microscope captures an electron micrograph of the flat top face.

8
00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:41.773
Then a thin slice is shaved away, exposing a fresh face below.

9
00:00:43.153 --> 00:00:46.993
Repeating this cycle creates a stack of consecutive micrographs.

10
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:51.113
Those flat images still have to become whole cells.

11
00:00:52.813 --> 00:00:57.833
Researchers trace each branch's outline across consecutive slices, combining manual

12
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:02.833
tracing with machine-learning segmentation, software that outlines cells

13
00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:08.953
automatically. Stacking those outlines rebuilds each neuron's branching tree, its

14
00:01:08.953 --> 00:01:13.993
arbor. Where reconstructed membranes meet, the map marks a contact.

15
00:01:13.993 --> 00:01:20.133
Together, the reconstructed neurons and chemical synapses form a connectome.

16
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:26.263
Now pause and look at one reconstructed branch meeting two neighbouring ganglion

17
00:01:26.263 --> 00:01:31.563
cells. One partner gets many contacts, while the neighbour gets far fewer.

18
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:37.663
Does a larger count prove a stronger synapse, or even prove every contact is a

19
00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:38.663
synapse?

20
00:01:43.617 --> 00:01:51.217
No. Serial slices reconstruct cells and contacts, yet a contact alone cannot prove a

21
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:54.417
synapse, and anatomy alone cannot prove function.

22
00:01:55.857 --> 00:02:00.997
Packed branches can touch without signalling, so a contact map proposes partners and

23
00:02:00.997 --> 00:02:01.997
does not prove them.

24
00:02:03.077 --> 00:02:07.817
A connectome also counts synapses without measuring strength or sign, meaning

25
00:02:07.817 --> 00:02:09.397
excitation or inhibition.

26
00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:12.837
Treating count as strength is an assumption.

27
00:02:14.017 --> 00:02:19.077
Nor can a wiring diagram alone say which direction a ganglion cell prefers.

28
00:02:20.437 --> 00:02:24.137
To guard against that limit, experimenters read function first.

29
00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:30.417
They image calcium in the retina, using moving bars to read each cell's preferred

30
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:34.777
direction. Then they cut and reconstruct that same tissue.

31
00:02:36.137 --> 00:02:40.837
Still, anatomy only observes; proving a pathway is needed takes a manipulation.

32
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.460
This approach unlocked real circuits.

33
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:51.280
Briggman, Helmstaedter and Denk imaged calcium using moving bars in wild-type mouse

34
00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.420
retina. They then cut that same tissue to reconstruct direction-selective ganglion

35
00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:59.060
cells and their contacts.

36
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:05.780
In 2013, Helmstaedter and colleagues paired manual tracing with machine-learning

37
00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:12.840
segmentation. They reconstructed 950 neurons and their mutual contacts in the mouse

38
00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:13.840
retina.

39
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:20.760
In 2018, Bae and colleagues used electron microscopy on a retinal patch whose light

40
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.100
responses were imaged.

41
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:28.880
Grouping cells by arbor shape and depth in the inner retina revealed 47 anatomical

42
00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.660
clusters and at least 35 types.

43
00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.680
A stack of slices maps who meets whom, while activity and manipulations reveal what

44
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:38.480
each circuit does.
